 film uh you obviously  wrote the book was this something that  almost was directed by somebody else  were you holding this back to be like  i'm i'm making this movie  i've been wanting to make a better  nathan ever filmed for a while but um i  think a lot of things had to happen i  had to have like the perfect cast and i  think you know ruby wood and aria  co-star in the film  that they they were the perfect age they  were both 14 when we shot it any other  time in the history of the world they  would have been too young or or they  would have outgrown it and then in my  experience show running high school  musical for the last three years you get  such a crash course in all things stress  and script writing and  time management and so it all sort of  converged at the same time to be uh i  think perfect timing  well listen this movie doesn't work  without uh ruby and i'm just what was it  like casting  and getting ruby because  were you nervous after casting him that  you're like we gotta start filming i  don't want him to grow six inches i  don't want his voice to change do you  know what i mean yeah i worked on the  show billy elliot on broadway and just  when the guys would go into the show and  and learn every dance and learn every  song almost a week later their voice  would change and so for a role like nate  who is right at the center of a  coming-of-age story about identity and  finding his people  you know i had to find a kid who really  represented that authentically the  biggest risk with ruby wood was that he  had never appeared on camera he's like a  real deal theater kid and i had a lot of  spirited conversations with disney uh he  had seven callbacks because i saw  something in him that was um  like a  diamond in the rough in in in every in  in every sense of that phrase he was not  yet shining perfectly but i knew he  would get there and and he was i thought  astonishing in the role oh ruby is very  good in the role uh there you you cast  uh uh you 